  "production_date": "about 1638",
  "brief_description": "Holding a quill in his right hand, the man in this portrait has turned his head towards us, creating the feeling that we have just interrupted his work. He gives us a questioning look, as if he is waiting for an explanation for the interruption. The sitter is Jean de la Chambre (1605/6--1668), a distinguished calligrapher and master of the French School in Haarlem.\n\nCompared to the ambitious large-scale portraits of members of the ruling classes for which Frans Hals is known, this is a relatively unassuming -- but nonetheless powerful -- work. It was painted as a model for an engraving executed by the Haarlem printmaker Jonas Suyderhoef. The engraving was used as the frontispiece for a book published by Jean de la Chambre in the same year, which contained six engraved plates of examples of his calligraphy.",
  "accession_date": "1972-01-01",
